  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes major phytoremediation pathways by showing a plant rooted in contaminated soil and highlighting how pollutants are handled in different plant compartments. At the bottom, heavy metal(loid)s are depicted around the roots, with a central contrast between “mobilization” and “immobilization,” indicating that root activity can either increase metal availability or lock contaminants in place in the rhizosphere.\n\nFrom this root zone, several remediation routes are illustrated. Phytoextraction is shown as red particles moving from soil into plant tissues, representing uptake and transport of contaminants into aboveground parts. Phytostabilization, in contrast, keeps those particles near the roots in soil, emphasizing containment rather than removal. Rhizodegradation is associated with microorganisms around the roots, suggesting that microbes and root interactions break down pollutants in the rhizosphere.\n\nIn the shoots and leaves, phytodegradation appears to show contaminants transformed within plant tissues, while phytovolatilization depicts small particles leaving the leaf surface to the air, indicating release of transformed volatile compounds. Overall, the image presents phytoremediation as a set of linked plant-based mechanisms that remove, transform, stabilize, or release contaminants depending on where and how the plant interacts with them.",
